<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  es-theme-xmb-psp - "Button Icons: Nintendo" (default).
  Selected via UI Settings > Theme Configuration > Button Icons > Nintendo.

  Default because the theme's primary target, the TrimUI Brick, silkscreens
  its face buttons in the Nintendo layout. A user who never opens Theme
  Configuration gets a help strip that matches the hardware in their hands
  (#8). PSP and Xbox stay one menu away.

  SLOT -> PHYSICAL BUTTON. Confirmed by render, not by reading THEMES.md.
  ES's four face-button icon slots are fixed to physical positions:

      iconA -> EAST     iconB -> SOUTH     iconX -> NORTH     iconY -> WEST

  Two independent sources agree. Devices configured from SDL's controller DB
  go through InputManager.cpp's _sdlToEsMapping, whose non-Windows branch maps
  SDL a(south)->ES "b", b(east)->ES "a", x(west)->ES "y", y(north)->ES "x";
  the Brick, which has an explicit es_input.cfg entry, records the same thing
  by evdev code (ES "a"=305/BTN_EAST, "b"=304/BTN_SOUTH, "x"=307/BTN_NORTH,
  "y"=308/BTN_WEST). InputConfig::buttonDisplayName agrees ("a"->EAST,
  "b"->SOUTH).

  The mapping does NOT depend on the InvertButtons setting. It is tempting to
  assume it does, because InputConfig::buttonLabel() — which is what picks the
  icon — contains an a/b swap keyed on InvertButtons. That swap is inside
  "#ifdef INVERTEDINPUTCONFIG", and InputConfig.h:12-14 defines
  INVERTEDINPUTCONFIG only "#ifdef WIN32". On the device and in the harness it
  is dead code, so buttonLabel() is the identity function and every slot is
  nailed to a position. InvertButtons still decides which POSITION confirms
  (BUTTON_OK = invertButtons ? "a" : "b"), so flipping it moves the labels
  CONFIRM/BACK between glyphs — it never moves a glyph between buttons. Both
  states were rendered; see scripts/tests/test-button-glyphs.sh.

  So for Nintendo the naive assignment is the correct one: east is A, south is
  B, north is X, west is Y. That is a coincidence of this layout, not a
  general rule — see buttons-xbox.xml, where every one of the four moves.

  Variables, not <helpsystem> properties, and deliberately so. A file that
  declared <view name="grid"> to reach the Box Art Grid's own helpsystem would
  make hasView("grid") true for every user, and ES would then build an
  unstyled built-in grid for anyone who had pinned Gamelist View Style = grid
  — the exact regression fixed in b5e2063. Variables register no views, and
  both helpsystem declarations (_inc/common.xml, _inc/gamelist-grid.xml) read
  the same four names, so all three gamelist styles stay in sync.

  CC-BY-NC-SA 2.0. See CREDITS.md.
-->
<theme>
  <formatVersion>7</formatVersion>
  <variables>
    <helpIconA>./art/help/btn-a.png</helpIconA>
    <helpIconB>./art/help/btn-b.png</helpIconB>
    <helpIconX>./art/help/btn-x.png</helpIconX>
    <helpIconY>./art/help/btn-y.png</helpIconY>
  </variables>
</theme>
